---
import DashboardLayout from '../layouts/DashboardLayout.astro';
import TopBar from '../components/TopBar.astro';
import ProsePage from '../components/ProsePage.astro';
import SearchModal from '../components/SearchModal.tsx';

export const prerender = true;
---

<DashboardLayout
  title="Privacy Policy | Claude Code Templates"
  description="What data aitmpl.com and the claude-code-templates CLI collect, why, who processes it, and how to opt out."
>
  <TopBar />
  <ProsePage heading="Privacy Policy" updated="November 8, 2026">
    <p>
      This page explains what data Claude Code Templates collects through the website at aitmpl.com and the
      <code>claude-code-templates</code> command-line tool, why, and how to opt out. The project does not sell personal data
      and does not use it for advertising profiles.
    </p>

    <h2>Browsing the website</h2>
    <p>
      You can browse the catalog without an account. Every page, including the catalog and blog articles under <code>/blog</code>, uses Google
      Analytics to count visits and see which pages people find useful, which sets Google cookies in your browser. The site
      loads no advertising trackers. Like any web server, our hosting provider (Cloudflare) processes your IP address and request details to
      deliver pages and protect the site from abuse. Your theme and layout preferences, the components you add to your stack,
      and a cached copy of the repository's GitHub star count are kept in your browser's local storage and never sent to us.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google Analytics also receives a few named events, so we can see which features people use: viewing a component,
      copying its install command, adding it to your stack, copying the install command of your stack or of a collection,
      clicking Save, opening and completing the sign-in, following a link to the component's source on GitHub, and searching.
      They carry only the component's type and path, the part of the site where it happened (for a sign-in, the feature that
      asked for it), a number of components, whether you were signed in, and for a search the number of results. They never
      carry what you type, a collection's name or your account details. When you open the sign-in, the feature that asked for
      it is kept in your browser's session storage so the completed sign-in can be counted; it is removed when the sign-in
      completes or the tab closes, and ignored after 30 minutes.
    </p>

    <h2>CLI usage statistics</h2>
    <p>
      When you install a component, the CLI sends a usage event so the site can show download counts and
      trending components. The event contains the component type, name and category, the CLI version, your Node.js version,
      operating system and architecture, whether the installation succeeded, how long it took, and a random identifier that
      groups the components installed in one run. When you install into a directory other than
      the current one, the event includes that directory relative to where you ran the command. When an installation fails,
      it includes the error message, which can contain local file paths. The contents of your files are never sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since version 1.32.0, the event also says whether the CLI is running inside an AI coding agent such as Claude Code,
      Cursor or Codex, and which one; how Claude Code was started (for example from a terminal, an editor or the SDK) when it is
      that agent; and whether the output goes to an interactive terminal. The CLI does not send the account email or the account
      and session identifiers that Claude Code makes available to it. Our server records the request's IP address, the country
      our hosting provider derives from it, and the user agent with download events.
    </p>
    <p>
      Commands that are not installations (the catalog browser, analytics, chats, the health check, workflows and options that
      were removed) send the command name, the CLI and Node.js versions, the operating system and architecture, and a random
      identifier created for each event. A workflow run also sends how many components of each type it contains, and a removed
      option sends its name.
    </p>
    <p>
      To install components, the CLI downloads files from GitHub (<code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code> and
      <code>api.github.com</code>) and from www.aitmpl.com, so those services receive your IP address, as with any download.
      Plugins are installed by Claude Code itself from the aitmpl marketplace at <code>www.aitmpl.com/marketplace.json</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To turn the usage events off, set <code>CCT_NO_TRACKING=true</code> (or <code>CCT_NO_ANALYTICS=true</code>) in your environment.
      Nothing is sent when <code>CI=true</code>. Crash reporting is a separate, off-by-default feature that only runs if you
      set <code>CCT_ERROR_REPORTING=true</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Accounts and collections</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in is optional for browsing the catalog and handled by Clerk. You need an account to save collections and to open
      job listings, certifications and courses. If you sign in, we store your account identifier and the component
      collections you create, including whether you made a collection public. Your name and email are held by Clerk, which also
      keeps a record of your sign-in sessions (device type, browser and approximate location).
    </p>

    <h2>Sending components to a GitHub repository</h2>
    <p>
      The "Send to repo" feature asks you to authorize our GitHub OAuth app. The resulting access token is kept in your
      browser's session storage, used only to create the commit or pull request you asked for, and is not stored on our servers.
    </p>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>
      From July to September 2026 we sent emails about new and trending components to people with an aitmpl.com
      account. The addresses came from the sign-in accounts held by Clerk; there was no separate sign-up form. Sending has been
      paused since September 20, 2026.
    </p>
    <p>
      We still keep each of those addresses, with its account identifier and unsubscribe status, in our database and with our
      email provider, Resend. We also keep the delivery events Resend reported for each recipient: opened, clicked (with the
      link), bounced or marked as spam. Every email included an unsubscribe link. To have your address and these records
      deleted, email <a href="mailto:daniel@aitmpl.com">daniel@aitmpl.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Sponsored placements</h2>
    <p>
      If you buy a sponsored placement, payment is handled by Stripe on admin.aitmpl.com. We keep a record of the placement
      and the Stripe payment reference; we never see or store your card details.
    </p>

    <h2>Error monitoring</h2>
    <p>
      Server errors on the website are reported to Sentry so we can fix them. Reports contain the error and the API route where it happened,
      not account data.
    </p>

    <h2>Service providers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Cloudflare: hosting and content delivery.</li>
      <li>Supabase and Neon: databases for usage statistics, collections, email records and placements.</li>
      <li>Clerk: sign-in.</li>
      <li>GitHub: OAuth for "Send to repo", the public source repository, and the files the CLI downloads.</li>
      <li>Resend: email delivery (paused since September 2026).</li>
      <li>Stripe: payments for sponsored placements.</li>
      <li>Sentry: error monitoring.</li>
      <li>Google: Google Fonts on every page, Google Analytics on every page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <p>
      You can delete your account and collections, ask what data we hold about you, or ask us to delete it, by emailing
      <a href="mailto:daniel@aitmpl.com">daniel@aitmpl.com</a>. We will answer within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page when what we collect changes, and the date above shows the latest version. The page's history
      is public in the <a href="https://github.com/davila7/claude-code-templates/commits/main/dashboard/src/pages/privacy.astro">GitHub repository</a>.
    </p>
  </ProsePage>
  <SearchModal client:idle />
</DashboardLayout>
